(footprint "UM232H"
	(version 20260206)
	(generator "pcbnew")
	(generator_version "10.0")
	(layer "F.Cu")
	(descr "FTDI UM232H USB Hi-Speed FT232H dev module -- 28-pin (2x14, 0.6in/15.24mm row spacing, 2.54mm pitch) THT DIP-style module, plugs into a standard 0.6in 28-pin DIP socket. Geometry derived directly from DS_UM232H.pdf Figure 3 (Module Dimensions): pin field is 33.02mm (13 x 2.54mm) long, starting 10.0mm from the USB-connector end of the 44.5mm-long, 18.25mm-wide board -- pin1 of each row sits at Y=-16.51 (10.0mm in from the top/USB-connector edge at Y=-26.51), pin14 at Y=+16.51 (1.48mm shy of the far edge at Y=+17.99). J2 is the left row (x=-7.62), J1 is the right row (x=+7.62), matching the module's own silkscreened orientation in Figure 2 (USB connector at top, J2 left/J1 right). Pad numbers are the datasheet's own J1-n/J2-n designators -- must match the UM232H symbol's pin numbers exactly. Drill/pad sized generously (1.0mm/1.8mm) for the module's 0.5mm round pins in a socket mount. Decorative/mechanical details shown in Fig 2/3 but not part of the J1/J2 signal interface (the extra unlabeled GND through-hole and crystal-shaped silkscreen icon near the bottom of the real board) are intentionally omitted. 3D model is lib/UM232H/UM232H.step, procedurally generated from Figure 3's dimensions by lib/UM232H/generate_step.py (not a vendor-supplied file -- see that script's docstring for every interpretive choice made). Two values are deliberate departures from Figure 3, per an explicit user request (not datasheet-derived): the pin length below the board is 8.0mm (Fig 3 itself says 14.9mm), and the model's own Z=0 is its pcb's BOTTOM surface (not top) so the footprint's (offset (xyz 0 0 2)) directly means the board bottom floats 2mm above the hub board, regardless of the hub board's actual thickness. The model is built in its own landscape orientation with its origin at the pin-field center, connector at -X. In an UNROTATED (0deg placement) footprint instance, (rotate (xyz 0 0 -90)) puts the connector at this footprint's local +Y end. IMPORTANT: this footprint is normally placed at a 180deg orientation on the actual hub board (see hub.kicad_pcb's own placed instance, its at-field's third value) to get the pad/silkscreen layout right for that design -- a 180deg PLACEMENT rotation adds directly to this model-block's own rotate (both are plain Z rotations), so the value that actually needs to sit in this file is whatever makes (model_rotate + placement_rotation) come out to the desired net orientation, not whatever looks right in an isolated 0deg test. That is why this field is (rotate (xyz 0 0 90)), not -90: net = 90 + 180 = 270 = -90, i.e. connector-at-local-+Y, same as an isolated -90 footprint -- if this part is ever placed at 0deg elsewhere, this rotate value alone will show the connector at local -Y (up on screen) and will need to go back to -90 for that placement. All of this (rotation sign, the placement-rotation interaction, the offset's host-thickness-independence, the exact 8mm pin span) is verified against KiCad's actual 3-D renderer, not just derived on paper or checked via `kicad-cli pcb export step` -- that export path was tried first and turned out to apply a DIFFERENT rotation convention than the real 3-D viewer/`pcb render`, producing a confidently-wrong rotation sign in an earlier version of this footprint; a second earlier version was checked via `pcb render` but only at 0deg placement, missing the 180deg-placement interaction entirely. The verification that stands behind the current values loads this exact footprint via pcbnew's Python API onto a throwaway board at the SAME position/orientation as the real hub.kicad_pcb instance (150.12, 134.03, 180deg) and runs `kicad-cli pcb render --side top/front` on it, then inspects the actual pixel positions of the rendered board/connector/pins: connector lands at the bottom of the screen (matching hub.kicad_pcb's own layout intent) and the board's own green PCB body (not the pin) sits measurably ~2.0mm above the host board's top surface for both a 1.6mm and a 0.8mm test stackup. A separately-modeled 1.27mm pin-housing collar (not datasheet-derived, not requested) was removed after an earlier render showed it swallowing most of that 2mm gap, making the correct offset look wrong at a glance; pins now run bare from the board's top surface to the tip.")
	(tags "FTDI UM232H FT232H USB DIP module")
	(property "Reference" "REF**"
		(at 0 -29 0)
		(layer "F.SilkS")
		(effects (font (size 1 1) (thickness 0.15)))
	)
	(property "Value" "UM232H"
		(at 0 21 0)
		(layer "F.Fab")
		(effects (font (size 1 1) (thickness 0.15)))
	)
	(attr through_hole)
	(fp_rect (start -9.125 -26.51) (end 9.125 17.99) (stroke (width 0.1) (type solid)) (layer "F.Fab"))
	(fp_rect (start -9.125 -26.51) (end 9.125 17.99) (stroke (width 0.12) (type solid)) (layer "F.SilkS"))
	(fp_rect (start -9.5 -26.9) (end 9.5 18.4) (stroke (width 0.05) (type solid)) (layer "F.CrtYd"))
	(fp_rect (start -4.0 -26.51) (end 4.0 -21.21) (stroke (width 0.1) (type solid)) (layer "F.Fab"))
	(fp_text user "J2" (at -7.62 -28.2 0) (layer "F.SilkS") (effects (font (size 1 1) (thickness 0.15))))
	(fp_text user "J1" (at 7.62 -28.2 0) (layer "F.SilkS") (effects (font (size 1 1) (thickness 0.15))))
	(fp_circle (center -10.4 -16.51) (end -10.1 -16.51) (layer "F.SilkS") (width 0.2))
	(fp_circle (center 10.4 -16.51) (end 10.1 -16.51) (layer "F.SilkS") (width 0.2))
	(pad "J2-1" thru_hole circle (at -7.62 -16.51) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J1-1" thru_hole circle (at 7.62 -16.51) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J2-2" thru_hole circle (at -7.62 -13.97) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J1-2" thru_hole circle (at 7.62 -13.97) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J2-3" thru_hole circle (at -7.62 -11.43) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J1-3" thru_hole circle (at 7.62 -11.43) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J2-4" thru_hole circle (at -7.62 -8.89) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
	(pad "J1-4" thru_hole circle (at 7.62 -8.89) (size 1.8 1.8) (drill 1.0) (layers "*.Cu" "*.Mask"))
